<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="EN">
	<head>
		{{ template "common_meta" . }}
		<link rel="stylesheet" type="text/css" href="/home.css">
	</head>
	
	<body>
		{{ template "common_heading_start" }}
		<h1>SwitchGopher</h1>
		{{ template "common_heading_end" }}
		<blockquote>
			go·fer<br>
			/ˈgōfər/<br>
			nouninformal<br>
			1. a person who runs errands, esp. on a movie set or in an office.
		</blockquote>
		<div class="line"><hr></div>
	
		<p>
			SwitchGopher keeps track of what is plugged into your network. It polls
			your switches over SNMP on a regular schedule and records each port's
			state, speed, VLAN and power draw, along with the devices seen on it:
			their MAC addresses, IP addresses and hostnames. Use the Devices page to
			browse switches by category, building or room, or search for a MAC address
			or hostname to find out where something is connected.
		</p>

		<p>
			The Vlans page shows how each VLAN is used, Stats gives network-wide totals,
			and Unused lists ports that have sat idle long enough to be reclaimed.
			SwitchGopher is a Go rewrite of the classic Perl tool switchmap. It only
			reads from your network and never changes a switch. New here? The Help page
			explains each page and how to read a switch's port table.
		</p>
		<br>
		<p>Happy switching!</p>
		<br>
		{{ template "common_footer" . }}
	</body>
</html>
